finding.evidence:
metrics.go:60-64 creates a map entry for whatever string it is handed, and nothing ever removes it:
	s := mt.stats[model]
	if s == nil {
		s = &ModelStat{lastSec: now}
		mt.stats[model] = s
	}
The key is the *raw client model name*. gateway.go:334 (inside Handle, before any resolution) does `ap.alias = cr.Model`; alias resolution only happens afterwards (gateway.go:335 `ups, ok := snap.Aliases[cr.Model]`, the strip retry at 339-346, the implicit-upstream retry at 351-354). The observation is emitted from the deferred block registered at gateway.go:314, i.e. it also fires on the reject paths: `s.mx.Observe(ap.alias, ap.status != "ok")` (gateway.go:318). The unknown-model 404 path returns at gateway.go:356-361 *before* the rate limiter (410) and quota (419) steps, so `s.mx.Observe("<arbitrary attacker string>", true)` runs on every one of those requests with no throttling and no counter check. The model string is never length- or charset-validated anywhere in the parse path: adapter.go:120-121 only tests emptiness (`if raw.Model == "" {` / `return nil, fmt.Errorf("model is required")`), and the body cap is 16 MiB (gateway.go:322), so a single key can be megabytes long. Repo-wide grep for `delete(mt.stats` -> 0 hits (compare slb.go:213-217, which *does* prune stale `bl.rr` cursors, proving the pattern exists elsewhere). The same unbounded string is also persisted per request: gateway.go:273 `Protocol: p.proto, ModelAlias: p.alias, ...` -> model.go:253 `gorm:"size:64;index" json:"model_alias"`, i.e. a megabyte value written into an indexed column that SQLite does not length-enforce. Finally the retained key is re-emitted to any caller on every Prometheus scrape as a label value (prom.go:136-138: the raw model string is interpolated into `promEsc(q.Model)` and printed twice per scrape).
